WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.370
SPEAKER: With almost 500
sessions at Next 2018,

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:04.620
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.170
So if you didn't see Using
Kubernetes, Spinnaker,

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.159
and Istio to Manage a
Multi-Cloud Environment,

00:00:09.159 --> 00:00:10.950
then stay tuned, because
here's the rewind.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:19.850
As a business you don't
want to put all of your eggs

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.160
into one cloud basket,
so it makes sense

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.960
to choose a
multi-cloud strategy.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.530
Or maybe your
developer and your boss

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.620
is telling you that you
have to go multi-cloud.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.453
Either way, you're faced
with the challenges

00:00:29.453 --> 00:00:32.180
of managing workloads and
resources across on premise

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:34.370
and multiple public clouds.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.590
There are lots of benefits
to going multi-cloud.

00:00:36.590 --> 00:00:39.240
You have the ability to optimize
based on cloud provider.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.510
You have less lock-in
and better availability

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.070
in case of any downtime.

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:44.450
However, all of
these differences

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.410
means a complex environment with
diverse tools and capabilities.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.780
Choice can lead to chaos,
and developers just

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.430
want the ability
to use a service.

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:55.070
Ideally, they can just
describe something they want,

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:56.750
and the resource
shows up for them.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.740
Abstraction is
valuable to developers,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.370
so we can walk through how
to make some of these tools,

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:04.410
develop and multi-cloud
approach to our application.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.920
Let's demonstrate how to
manage container orchestration,

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.830
continuous integration and
deployment, and a service mesh

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.511
with this focus of abstraction.

00:01:11.511 --> 00:01:13.010
It's also important
to note that you

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:15.680
can use any of your existing
tools and swap out for these.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.660
These fit well for
multi-cloud, but you

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.540
can decide which are best
for your application.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:22.040
Starting with
Kubernetes, you can

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.897
use this to coordinate your
containers in a consistent way.

00:01:24.897 --> 00:01:27.230
This means that you get a
separate business logic, which

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.032
is the code that runs
the important stuff,

00:01:29.032 --> 00:01:30.740
from having to manage
the services, which

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:32.450
is how it runs in the cloud.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.370
This abstraction helps
you focus on describing

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.770
how the workload
should behave and scale

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.470
without needing to worry about
the platform or any hardware

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:41.060
specifics.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.860
Once your application
can run, you'll

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.650
need to manage deployments
for releasing new versions.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:48.050
Instead of pushing out a new
version to all of your users

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:50.510
at once, you can use a canary
build to release it out

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.430
to a limited number of users
and understand the impact.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.060
In fact, there are a number
of ways to manage application

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:56.990
deployment.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.300
Spinnaker gives you
application-centric controls

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.760
for all stages of the
application lifecycle.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.950
You can create pipelines
to handle deployments,

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:07.370
rolling updates, version
tests, and do smooth rollbacks.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:08.840
Spinnaker is open
source and works

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.610
with a variety of
extensions to fit

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:12.810
into your lifecycle
management and workflows.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.226
Now that you've
got an application

00:02:14.226 --> 00:02:16.790
running and updating smoothly
across multiple environments,

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:19.165
you need to make sure that
your business logic abstracted

00:02:19.165 --> 00:02:21.000
from your network functionality.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.605
Istio, another community-driven
open source effort,

00:02:23.605 --> 00:02:24.980
helps you manage
your application

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:26.600
by doing exactly that.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.520
This builds on the
theme from before--

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:31.910
give app developers more
time to focus on core logic

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:34.460
and abstract away all
the advanced networking.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.830
By using Istio, you can
configure communication

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:39.650
between services, enforce
security and resiliency,

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:41.260
and monitor networking.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.350
Istio is made up of configurable
components such as Envoy,

00:02:44.350 --> 00:02:46.100
Pilot, Mixer, and Citadel.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:49.250
It's a lot to take in, but
Ameer does a fantastic job

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.140
of showcasing what
these components do

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:53.540
and how they give you full
control of your application

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.470
networking for smarter
routing, higher

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.770
resiliency, strong
policy enforcement,

00:02:57.770 --> 00:02:59.192
and observable metrics.

00:02:59.192 --> 00:03:00.650
I definitely
recommend checking out

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.632
the walkthrough of Istio's
architecture for more details.

00:03:03.632 --> 00:03:05.090
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:06.840
check out the link in
the description below.

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.923
And don't forget to subscribe
to the cloud YouTube

00:03:08.923 --> 00:03:11.160
